This study investigated the relationship between neck pain severity and health-related quality of life (HRQoL) 6 months later in a population-based cohort. The outcomes measured were the physical and mental components of HRQoL using the SF-36 questionnaire. The findings indicate that there was an exposure-response relationship between the severity of neck pain and physical HRQoL, with participants experiencing mild, intense, or disabling neck pain at baseline reporting worse physical HRQoL 6 months later, even after adjusting for confounding factors. However, there was no significant association found between neck pain and mental HRQoL.

For the worsening course of neck pain or persistent neck pain, these trajectories were specifically linked to worse physical HRQoL 6 months later. Thus, the evidence supports a negative relationship between neck pain severity and future physical HRQoL.

